---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SocialLinks from "../components/SocialLinks.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="A small shop for a large TBR"
  description="What Oli's Bookshelf publishes, and the rules that keep reading-order guides honest."
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>A small shop for a large TBR</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Long series punish first-time readers. Prequels spoil. Novellas look skippable. “Complete”
      lists quietly include picture books and RPG extras. This site exists to put the path on the
      counter.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>What we publish</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/guides/">Reading-order guides</a>, short{" "}
      <a href="/questions/">question pages</a>, and “what to read next” notes for science
      fiction, fantasy, mystery, and romance series that still confuse people. Publication order
      first. Chronology when it diverges. We do not retell novels or quote copyrighted prose.
    </p>

    <h2>Editorial rules</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Facts over flavor.</strong> Titles, years, and “this story sits before that novel”
        need a source. Author and publisher pages beat fandom wikis.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No scraped blurbs.</strong> If a retailer description spoils, we say so without
        repeating the spoiler.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Drafts stay labeled.</strong> Work in progress ships as <code>status: draft</code>
        until titles, dates, and flags are checked. Published guides on this site are marked
        <code>reviewed</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Next-reads are arguments.</strong> Each suggestion needs a reason — voice,
        structure, aftercare — not a star average.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No fake reviews.</strong> No invented quotations, Goodreads voices, or tears on a
        page number.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      The full sourcing method is on the <a href="/methodology/">methodology</a> page. Dates on
      each guide are the last time we opened the listed sources.
    </p>

    <h2>Who this is not for</h2>
    <p>
      If you want recaps, leaked manuscripts, or a course on “book blogs that print money,” this is
      the wrong shop. High-search series are in scope when the order is still contested — including{" "}
      <a href="/guides/lord-of-the-rings-reading-order/">Tolkien</a>,{" "}
      <a href="/guides/harry-potter-reading-order/">Harry Potter</a>, and other paths people start
      on the wrong book. The <a href="/guides/">full catalog</a> is grouped by shelf.
    </p>

    <h2>Find Oli</h2>
    <p>
      The books channel and this reading-order site are the same shop: same name, same shelves.
      Videos live on YouTube. The maps live here.
    </p>
    <SocialLinks />
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
